  "body": "## Summary\n[`_processLimitBidOrder()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-07-gte-clob/blob/2783108d14010ae50f2a5f65c3a9c94e2e7845b9/contracts/clob/CLOB.sol#L492-L531) and [`_processLimitAskOrder()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-07-gte-clob/blob/2783108d14010ae50f2a5f65c3a9c94e2e7845b9/contracts/clob/CLOB.sol#L533-L571) use a bit-wise AND (`&`) instead of a logical check to detect zero-amount trades, causing legitimate trades to revert with `ZeroCostTrade`.\n\n## Finding Description  \nInside `CLOB.sol`, both `_processLimitBidOrder()` (line 503) and `_processLimitAskOrder()` (line 544) contain:\n\n```solidity\nif (baseTokenAmountReceived != quoteTokenAmountSent && baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent == 0) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\n\nThe developer’s intention is to throw when either side of the trade is zero.  \nInstead, `baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent` performs a bit-level comparison. Two **non-zero** integers that share no common set bits (e.g., `1` and `2`) pass this test, so the compound condition becomes true whenever the amounts differ and have no overlapping bits. In practice:\n\n1. Maker places a sell (ASK) limit order.  \n2. Taker submits a matching buy (BID) with `amountInBase = 1` and `quoteAmount = 2`.  \n3. `1 != 2` is true, `1 & 2 == 0` is also true ⇒ `ZeroCostTrade()` reverts, aborting the otherwise valid match.\n\nBecause base amounts are denominated in base tokens and quote amounts in quote tokens, equality is virtually impossible, so every trade relies on the bit-wise clause. Random bit patterns therefore decide whether normal trades succeed or fail, enabling denial-of-service on any pair of amounts with disjoint bits.\n\n## Impact Explanation  \nHigh – attacker can systematically revert genuine matches, effectively freezing order execution and denying service.\n\n\n## Recommendation  \nReplace the bit-wise comparison with an explicit zero test",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add the test file into `test/clob/unit/clob`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\n// The base test utilities already deploy the full CLOB stack and give us helper\n// functions + pre-funded users.\nimport {CLOBTestBase} from \"test/clob/utils/CLOBTestBase.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\n\n/// @title PoC for ZeroCostTrade bitwise-AND bug\n/// @notice Demonstrates that a perfectly valid trade reverts with ZeroCostTrade\n///         because `baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent == 0` can be\n///         true for **non-zero** values when they share no common set bits.\ncontract CLOBZeroCostTradeTest is CLOBTestBase {\n    /// @notice Finds the smallest price (multiple of `TICK_SIZE`) that makes\n    ///         `amountInBase & quoteAmount == 0` while both are non-zero.\n    function _findProblematicPrice(uint256 amountInBase) internal pure returns (uint256 price) {\n        uint256 tick = TICK_SIZE; // 1e14 wei (0.0001 ether)\n        // Scan a generous range of ticks to deterministically find a pair\n        // whose bit patterns do not overlap. 100k iterations is cheap in a\n        // test context (well under the block gas limit) and guarantees success\n        // for normal token sizes.\n        for (uint256 i = 1; i < 100_000; ++i) {\n            price = tick * i;\n            uint256 quote = (amountInBase * price) / BASE_TOKEN_SIZE;\n            if (quote == 0) continue; // need non-zero quote\n            if ((amountInBase & quote) == 0) {\n                return price;\n            }\n        }\n        revert(\"price search failed - enlarge search range\");\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Reproduces the revert due to the incorrect bitwise AND logic.\n    function testZeroCostTradeReverts() public {\n        // Choose the minimum order size to keep numbers small & deterministic\n        uint256 amountInBase = MIN_LIMIT_ORDER_AMOUNT_IN_BASE; // 0.005 ether\n        uint256 price = _findProblematicPrice(amountInBase);\n\n        // Sanity check the helper really found a suitable price\n        uint256 quoteAmount = (amountInBase * price) / BASE_TOKEN_SIZE;\n        assertTrue(amountInBase != quoteAmount, \"amounts should differ\");\n        assertTrue((amountInBase & quoteAmount) == 0, \"bitwise AND must be zero\");\n\n        // 1. Maker posts an ASK (sell) order that will later be matched.\n        address maker = users[0];\n        setupTokens(Side.SELL, maker, amountInBase, price, true);\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory askArgs = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: NEVER,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n\n        vm.prank(maker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(maker, askArgs);\n\n        // 2. Taker attempts to BUY the same amount – this should revert *only*\n        //    because of the buggy `ZeroCostTrade` check.\n        address taker = users[1];\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, taker, amountInBase, price, true);\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory bidArgs = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price, // match maker's price so it is immediately filled\n            cancelTimestamp: NEVER,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.ZeroCostTrade.selector);\n        vm.prank(taker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(taker, bidArgs);\n    }\n}\n\n```",
